    "content": "一根均勻的槓桿長 1.2 米，支點設於左端起 0.3 米處。左端掛上 60 N 的重物。\n（a）若在右端施力使槓桿平衡，所需的力為多少？\n（b）此槓桿的機械利益（mechanical advantage）為多少？\n（c）說明此結果反映了槓桿設計上的哪一項取捨。",
    "explanation": "（c）才是本題的考點。力矩計算屬程序操作，而能否由結果讀出設計含意，才是本科與物理科的分別所在。機械利益高並非無條件的優點 —— 它以行程換取力量，而在空間受限的裝置（例如手提工具、車廂內的機構）之中，行程本身可能就是最緊的約束。\n計算部分須留意力臂的量度起點是支點，而非槓桿的端點。把 1.2 米整條當成施力臂是最常見的錯誤，得出的答案為 15 N，看似合理而實際偏低。\n作答時把「功不變」這一點寫出來，比只說「省力但要移動較遠」更能顯示理解其原理。",
    "referenceAnswer": "（a）力臂：負載臂 = 0.3 m；施力臂 = 1.2 − 0.3 = 0.9 m。\n取矩平衡：60 × 0.3 = F × 0.9\nF = 18 ÷ 0.9 = 20 N。\n（b）機械利益 = 負載 ÷ 施力 = 60 ÷ 20 = 3。\n（亦可由力臂之比得出：0.9 ÷ 0.3 = 3。）\n（c）取捨在於【力】與【距離】之間。\n施力只需 20 N 即可支撐 60 N 的負載，省力三倍；但施力端須移動的距離亦為負載端的三倍。若要把重物提升 0.1 米，施力端須下移 0.3 米。\n機械裝置並不會創造能量，它只改變力與距離的分配 —— 省了力就必須多走距離，兩者的乘積（即功）在理想情況下維持不變。設計時究竟該偏向省力抑或偏向省行程，取決於實際限制：空間狹窄者宜取短行程，操作者力量有限者宜取高機械利益。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Load arm 0.3 m, effort arm 0.9 m. Taking moments: 60 × 0.3 = F × 0.9, so F = 20 N.\n(b) Mechanical advantage = 60/20 = 3 (or 0.9/0.3 = 3).\n(c) The trade-off is between force and distance. Only 20 N supports a 60 N load, a threefold saving in effort, but the effort end must travel three times as far: raising the load 0.1 m requires the effort end to move 0.3 m.\nA mechanism creates no energy; it redistributes force and distance, and what is saved in force must be paid in travel, the product of the two — the work done — remaining unchanged in the ideal case. Whether a design should favour low effort or short travel depends on the constraint: short travel where space is tight, high mechanical advantage where the operator’s strength is limited.",
    "contentEn": "A uniform lever 1.2 m long has its fulcrum 0.3 m from the left end, with a 60 N load hung at that end.\n(a) What force applied at the right end balances the lever?\n(b) What is the mechanical advantage?\n(c) What design trade-off does this result illustrate?",
    "explanationEn": "Part (c) is the point of the question. Taking moments is routine; reading a design implication out of the result is what separates this subject from physics. High mechanical advantage is not an unqualified good — it buys force with travel, and in space-constrained devices such as hand tools or in-vehicle mechanisms travel may be the binding constraint.\nIn the calculation, arms are measured from the fulcrum, not from the ends of the lever. Treating the full 1.2 m as the effort arm is the commonest error, giving 15 N, which looks plausible but is too low.\nStating that the work done is unchanged shows more understanding than saying only that effort is saved at the cost of distance."
