    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) First, define and research: interview wheelchair users and observe how they actually open doors; measure seat height, reach range and the maximum torque applicable one-handed; consult accessible design standards. The output is a brief stating the constraints.\nSecond, generate ideas: produce several distinct directions against those constraints — lever, push plate, sensor-operated, extended lever — valuing quantity over polish so as not to narrow too early.\nThird, select and develop: evaluate against set criteria (force required, reach height, cost, durability, whether the door itself must be altered), take one or two forward, produce working drawings and choose materials.\nFourth, prototype: build an operable sample from available materials, judged on whether it can be used rather than on finish.\nFifth, test and refine: have real users try it in a real setting, record performance and problems, revise and test again. This stage is not once-only; the process is a cycle, not a line.\n(b) First, the designer’s body differs from the user’s. Someone who can stand and use both hands cannot imagine seated reach height, the real limit of one-handed force, or how the position of chair relative to door constrains movement. These are measurable facts, not guessable preferences.\nSecond, real use contains steps a designer does not anticipate. Opening a door for a wheelchair user is not one action but approach, position, open, pass through, close — and whether the door swings back during passage often matters more than whether it opens at all, which only observation reveals.\nThird, users differ among themselves in upper-limb strength, hand function and chair type, so a single imagined user yields a design that suits one case.\nAn example failure: placing the handle at a standard height of about 1.0 m is entirely reasonable from a standing viewpoint, but a wheelchair user’s comfortable operating range is roughly 0.8 to 1.1 m with forward reach to allow for, and if there is too little clear space beside the door for the chair to come alongside, the user cannot reach a position from which force can be applied at all. Such failures do not appear on a drawing, only in field testing.\n(c) The conclusion does not hold.\nFirst, it confuses \"can be done\" with \"designed well\". Accessible design does not aim at letting users just manage but at letting them do it as conveniently as anyone else. A markedly slower operation still transfers a cost — time, effort, others waiting — onto the user, which is exactly what accessible design exists to remove.\nSecond, time is a functional criterion, not an incidental comfort. In real settings people may be waiting behind the door, the user may feel pressure and avoid that route, and a facility that takes too long is often barely used, which is equivalent to not providing it.\nThird, the test result points to the next revision rather than confirming completion. Testing is not a pass-or-fail verdict but a way of finding where the problem lies. \"Opens but slowly\" is a specific, traceable clue — slow at which step? Unstable grip, poor point of application, or a need to reposition the chair? The next step is to break the sequence down and address the bottleneck.\nThe fifth stage contains the word \"repeat\". Settling on the first test result treats a cyclical process as a linear one.",
    "contentEn": "A student designs a door handle operable one-handed by a wheelchair user.\n(a) List the five main stages of the design process and state what each involves in this case.\n(b) Explain why user research cannot be replaced by the designer’s own imagination, and give one failure that could result from omitting it.\n(c) A prototype is tested: users can open the door but take longer than usual. The student concludes that since the function works, the design is settled. Comment.",
    "explanationEn": "Marks in (a) do not come from reciting five stage names but from what each involves in this case. \"Carry out research\" merely repeats the stage name; \"measure seat height and the torque applicable one-handed\" is the content sought.\nThe second reason in (b) is worth noting: the action a designer imagines usually has fewer steps than real use. Opening a door is one action to an able-bodied observer and five in a wheelchair user’s actual sequence — and the problems usually lie in the middle steps, precisely those imagination skips.\nPart (c) illustrates a core criterion in this subject: achieving the function is not completing the design. This holds especially in accessible design, where the gap between barely usable and equally usable is the reason the field exists. The closing point that the process is cyclical ties (c) back to the fifth stage in (a).",
    "markingScheme": "（a）5 分 —— 五個階段各 1 分，須連本個案的具體工作；只列階段名稱者全項最多 2 分。\n（b）2 分 —— 至少兩個理由，須指向不同層面（1）；具體失誤例子須說明何以在圖紙上不可見（1）。\n（c）2 分 —— 指出功能達成不等於設計完成，或時間本身即功能指標（1）；指出測試結果應導向下一輪改良，呼應設計過程的循環性（1）。答「應該再測試」而無理由不給分。\n本題所列分數為本平台練習用尺度，並非香港考試及評核局的分數分配。參考答案只列必達步驟與判準，刻意不提供可直接抄錄的完整答卷。交卷後請逐項對照自評，機器不會為本題評分。",
    "markingSchemeEn": "(a) 5 marks — one per stage, each with case-specific content; a bare list of stage names caps the whole part at 2.\n(b) 2 marks — at least two reasons at different levels (1); a concrete failure explained as invisible on a drawing (1).\n(c) 2 marks — the function-versus-design point, or time as a functional criterion (1); the test result read as directing the next iteration, echoing the cyclical process (1). \"Test again\" without reasons earns nothing.\nThe marks shown are a practice scale used on this platform, not an HKEAA mark allocation. The reference answer lists the required steps and criteria rather than a copyable model script. Compare your own work against it point by point; nothing here is machine-marked.",
